The best approach for `File.*` and `Directory.*` is to adopt the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's battle-tested and already provides `IFileSystem` with a mock-friendly test helper.

## Setup

**1. Install the package in your main project:**
```
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
```

**2. Install the test helper in your test project:**
```
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

**3. Register in DI** (in `Program.cs` or `Startup.cs`):
```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

## Usage

**4. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes** instead of calling statics directly:

```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions;

public class ConfigManager(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Load(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Save(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool EnsureDirectory(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

## Testing

**5. Use `MockFileSystem` in tests** — no real disk I/O needed:

```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers;

var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});

var manager = new ConfigManager(mockFs);

// Test reading
Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", manager.Load("/config.json"));

// Test writing
manager.Save("/output.txt", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/output.txt"));

// Test directory existence
Assert.False(manager.EnsureDirectory("/nonexistent"));
```

## Why this over a custom wrapper?

| Approach | Verdict |
|----------|---------|
| Custom `IFileWrapper` | ❌ You'll end up re-inventing the wheel for every new `File.*`/`Directory.*` method |
| `System.IO.Abstractions` | ✅ Covers `File`, `Directory`, `Path`, `DriveInfo`, etc. with full mock support |

Once the package is installed, use the `migrate-static-to-wrapper` skill (or do it manually) to bulk-replace your existing `File.ReadAllText(...)` → `fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(...)` call sites and add constructor injection.
